# deploy/anchor — the §9 publisher and its kubo.
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#
# If you are running a resolver, a delegate, the service or settlement, you are
# in the wrong directory — `docs/runbooks/operators.md`.
#
# ── THE PUBLISHER KEY IS NOT IN THIS FILE ───────────────────────────────────
# Same rule as `deploy/settlement`: export it into the shell that runs
# `docker compose up`, from a secret manager, a systemd credential, or your own
# store. The compose file declares it `:?`, so a missing key stops `up` with a
# message naming the variable.
#
#   set -a; . ~/.nns/evm-keys.env; set +a
#   export NNS_ANCHOR_PUBLISHER_KEY=$NNS_ANCHOR_PUBLISHER_KEY_1
#
# §9 says this SHOULD be a multisig signer. It is the repo's one hot EVM key.

# ── The anchor chain (required, never defaulted) ────────────────────────────
# A defaulted chain id would agree with a stale RPC URL, and anchoring to the
# wrong network succeeds silently.
#
#   Sepolia      11155111    — rehearse here first
#   Polygon PoS  137         — production
NNS_ANCHOR_RPC_URL=https://ethereum-sepolia-rpc.publicnode.com
NNS_ANCHOR_CHAIN_ID=11155111

# Where NnsAnchor lives — an output of `pnpm --filter @nns/anchor deploy`,
# and verified against the committed artifact on every run before anything is
# sent, so a wrong address is a refusal rather than a silent no-op.
NNS_ANCHOR_CONTRACT_ADDRESS=

# The NNS API serving /log and /checkpoints/{height}. Any operator's — the
# publisher anchors what it reads, and reading someone else's is a legitimate
# thing to do.
NNS_ANCHOR_API_URL=https://resolver.example.com

# ── Implementation B (required — A is the bundled kubo) ─────────────────────
# §8.2 wants a genuinely *independent second implementation*, and the snapshot
# is anchored only when both mint the same CID. Set exactly one of the Filebase
# block or IPFS_ADD_URL_2; both is refused, and two URLs that are the same
# endpoint is refused.
#
# Filebase is the named default: it runs its own importer and reports the
# minted CID in `x-amz-meta-cid`. The bucket must be IPFS-backed.
NNS_ANCHOR_FILEBASE_BUCKET=
NNS_ANCHOR_FILEBASE_KEY=
NNS_ANCHOR_FILEBASE_SECRET=
#NNS_ANCHOR_FILEBASE_ENDPOINT=https://s3.filebase.com
#
# Alternative: a second kubo-RPC-shaped endpoint that is NOT the same
# implementation as A. The publisher refuses identical URLs, but only you can
# make the implementations independent — two kubos of the same version are one
# implementation run twice, and would agree on a bug.
#NNS_ANCHOR_IPFS_ADD_URL_2=
#NNS_ANCHOR_IPFS_ADD_AUTH_2=

# ── §11.5, in EVM clothing (required) ───────────────────────────────────────
# Refuses to sign when the balance cannot cover one anchor; warns below this
# while still anchoring. 0.1 POL = 100000000000000000 wei.
NNS_ANCHOR_MIN_BALANCE_WEI=10000000000000000

# ── Optional ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# How often the publisher LOOKS, not how often it anchors — §9's cadence is on
# change with a daily floor, decided by the publisher itself. Default 4 h.
#NNS_ANCHOR_INTERVAL_SECONDS=14400

# Set to --dry-run to plan without sending. With it you may supply
# NNS_ANCHOR_PUBLISHER_ADDRESS instead of the key, and the whole plan runs on a
# machine that has never held it.
#NNS_ANCHOR_SEND=--send
#NNS_ANCHOR_PUBLISHER_ADDRESS=

# How far back eth_getLogs looks for this publisher's own anchors, for
# idempotency and gap reporting. Default 100000 (~2.3 days on Polygon PoS).
# **Lower it on public Sepolia endpoints**, which commonly cap getLogs at
# 50,000 blocks and answer a wider range with an error rather than a truncation.
#NNS_ANCHOR_LOOKBACK_BLOCKS=45000

#NNS_ANCHOR_IPFS_ADD_AUTH=
